{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778869844,"nonce":"1Bk9YSN5PDsJ0QpiV7iVE4PkrMA=","message":"Mandible already has the beginnings of both systems:\n\n- The Graph tab exists and is backed by `GET /api/graph`.\n- Current graph nodes are `issue`, `session`, and `auth_request`.\n- Current graph edges are `session_issue`, `session_parent`, `auth_issue`, and partially planned `umbrella_child`.\n- The current UI uses a Preact view (`ui/src/views/graph.tsx`) and a D3 SVG canvas (`ui/src/components/graph/GraphCanvas.tsx`).\n- The Issues tab already has a strong detail surface: issue body, timeline, work unit, related sessions/auth requests, and action buttons.\n- Work units already join issues, branches, sessions, and auth requests into a lifecycle model.\n- The project registry/work scanner already gives Mandible persistent repo identities through `work_repos`.\n- Auth request rows already carry repo, branch, issue IDs, commit messages, request type, execution target/session/repo scoping, and handoff metadata.\n- Provider/artifact support is mostly conceptual today. There is no durable provider registry, artifact store, manifest ingest API, or provider action registry yet.\n\nThe current graph should be treated as a prototype projection of a broader project graph, not as the final graph model. Its main limitation is that graph shape is hardcoded in the route and renderer: node types, edge types, filters, navigation, styling, and detail behavior are not yet data-driven enough to support provider nodes, artifact lineage, curated semantic edges, or multiple subgraph modes.\n\n\nMandible should grow one canonical project graph substrate, then expose multiple projections of that substrate:\n\n- Graph workspace: exploratory canvas with subgraph modes, clustering, previews, detail panes, and actions.\n- Issue view: list/detail projection over issue-centered subgraphs.\n- Auth view: queue/history projection over auth/action-request subgraphs.\n- Sessions/conversations views: time and conversation projections over session/conversation subgraphs.\n- Provider/artifact views: provider-specific detail projections over specs, manifests, runs, artifacts, reports, and provider actions.\n\nThis means the Graph tab should not merely accumulate special-case node colors. It should become a reusable graph workspace backed by typed nodes/edges, queryable subgraphs, action descriptors, and detail descriptors. Existing tabs may remain useful as dense task-specific projections, but they should increasingly share the same underlying entity and relationship model.\n\n\nThese requirements are intentionally strong. Implementations may choose different internal factoring, but should not weaken these properties without an explicit design comment.\n\n- Mandible must maintain stable identities for graph entities across repos, sessions, issues, auth requests, provider manifests, artifacts, specs, runs, reports, commits, and branches.\n- Graph nodes and edges must be typed, versioned, and carry enough metadata for rendering, filtering, navigation, preview, and provider action compatibility.\n- The graph API must distinguish exhaustive provenance edges from curated semantic edges.\n- The graph workspace must support subgraph modes rather than one hardcoded `/api/graph` shape.\n- The graph workspace must support selected-node detail panes and hover previews; click-through navigation alone is not sufficient.\n- Provider-specific actions must be data-driven from provider capabilities and selected graph node types, not hardcoded per provider in the Mandible UI.\n- Artifact records must be stable and content-addressed where possible; large files should not be copied into git repos merely to preserve lineage.\n- Provider manifests and imported artifacts must create graph nodes and provenance edges.\n- Mandible must be able to ingest provider manifests without understanding the provider's domain semantics in detail.\n- Providers must own domain validation and domain execution; Mandible owns orchestration, review/auth, artifacts, provenance, and cross-project memory.\n- The system must remain local-first. Remote stores and remote execution can be added, but a local filesystem/SQLite path must be viable.\n- Graph and provider work must not make Feedbax a hard dependency of Mandible.\n\n\n- This does not require replacing Mandible's whole UI stack immediately.\n- This does not require eliminating the Issues, Auth, Sessions, Conversations, Work, Presets, or Switchboard tabs.\n- This does not require a full knowledge-graph/RDF system in the first implementation.\n- This does not require every artifact to be immutable forever; retention policy can be explicit.\n- This does not require provider execution in the first slice. Open, validate, ingest, and publish are enough for MVP.\n- This does not require Mandible to understand Feedbax component/task/loss schemas internally.\n\n\n**Entity**: A durable object Mandible can identify. Examples: repo, issue, work unit, session, conversation, auth request, commit, branch, provider, provider spec, run manifest, analysis manifest, report, artifact.\n\n**Graph node**: A projection of an entity into a graph response. A node should include entity identity, type, labels, status, display hints, preview fields, and supported actions.\n\n**Graph edge**: A typed relationship between two entities. Some are observed provenance; others are curated semantic assertions.\n\n**Subgraph mode**: A named query/projection such as issue graph, repo graph, work graph, provenance graph, provider lineage graph, artifact lineage graph, or curated project graph.\n\n**Provider**: A domain tool registered with Mandible that exposes capabilities, schemas, validation, execution, UI entry points, artifact roles, and manifest types.\n\n**Artifact**: A large or non-source object tracked by Mandible, such as a checkpoint, trajectory array, rendered report, table, figure, log, or provider manifest bundle.\n\n**Provider action**: A declared operation that can be shown on compatible graph nodes, such as open in Feedbax Studio, validate spec, run analysis, publish report, upload artifact, or start reviewed compute.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}